{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1872 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "129ce0383f65a4d1517884f5ec240d71acdca77b9b6e297378f75eb129e6be9c" :edb/db-before-t 1871 :edb/db-after-t 1872 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535184 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:56:46.351Z" 13194139535184 true] [17592186147276 1100 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186147276 1100 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$r(t) = 300t^2+50$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour.\n\n1. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n2. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." 13194139535184 false] [17592186147276 1145 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\nTo find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2$, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}300t^{2} + 50dt \\\\\n&= 100t^{3} + 50t \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (100 \\cdot 2^{3} + 50(2)) - (0) \\\\\n&= (800 + 100) \\\\\n&= 900\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\npeople per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t)$, is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}R(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t) - 25dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt_(⏟)_(900) - ∫_{0}^{2}25dt \\\\\n&= 900 - [25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 900 - 50 \\\\\n&= 850\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." 13194139535184 false] [17592186147276 1145 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." 13194139535184 true] [17592186147277 1100 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186147277 1100 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 6t^2+2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" 13194139535184 false] [17592186147277 1145 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} + 2 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= T(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}R(x)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= 50 + ∫_{0}^{1}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + [2x^{3} - 4x] \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 50 + [(2(1)^{3} - 4(1)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= 50 - 2 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535184 false] [17592186147277 1145 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186147278 1100 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." 13194139535184 true] [17592186147278 1100 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$T(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,$.\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0$, find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." 13194139535184 false] [17592186147278 1145 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(50) + \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}\n$$\n\nand $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0$,\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nmust be the minimum value." 13194139535184 false] [17592186147278 1145 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value." 13194139535184 true] [17592186147338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336083 13194139535184 true] [17592186147338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336086 13194139535184 true] [17592186147338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336089 13194139535184 true] [17592186147338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336132 13194139535184 true] [17592186147339 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336093 13194139535184 true] [17592186147339 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336096 13194139535184 true] [17592186147339 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336111 13194139535184 true] [17592186147339 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336118 13194139535184 true] [17592186147339 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336125 13194139535184 true] [17592186147340 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336099 13194139535184 true] [17592186147340 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336105 13194139535184 true] [17592186147340 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336139 13194139535184 true] [17592186147340 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336146 13194139535184 true] [17592186147954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186147954 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 20e^{-2t}$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up.\n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t = 1$ minute and $t = 3$ minutes?" 13194139535184 false] [17592186147954 1100 "A water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function \n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up. \n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186147954 1145 "We're given the **rate of change** of volume $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in volume between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}20{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{20}{2}{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={-10{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-10({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & ≈1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes, $1.3$ liters of water entered the tank." 13194139535184 true] [17592186147963 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186147963 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$T(t) = 10 + ∫_{0}^{t}2x - 4dx$,\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?" 13194139535184 false] [17592186147963 1100 "The total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186147963 1145 "To find when the number of people is at a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2t-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n2t-4=0\\,⟹\\,t=2\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$ To find out how many people are in the festival when $t=2,$ we figure out $T(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}-4x]|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+[({2}^{2}-4(2))-(0)] \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6\\,000<10\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $6\\,000$, and this will happen after $2$ hours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336084 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1089 "q-349752" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1100 "A laboratory is tracking the rate at which a chemical is being produced in a reaction. The rate $r(t)$ at which the chemical is produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in milligrams per hour, and $0<t<5$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{pm}.$\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} milligrams were produced." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1113 #uuid "ba53887f-6b38-5495-ab47-7768f2a88cd4" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336083 1145 "We're given the **rate of change** of chemical production $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of chemical produced by the reaction between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{3}{3}{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{4}-(3)^{3}+4(3))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}-(1)^{3}+4(1)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{81}{2}-27+12)-(\\frac{1}{2}-1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, $22$ milligrams were produced." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336085 13194139535184 true] [17592186336084 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336084 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336084 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336085 13194139535184 true] [17592186336084 1110 #uuid "5c6fb7b5-de3b-53c0-bf16-23243a0bf349" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336085 1034 "22" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336085 1111 #uuid "da9fff78-dc71-5410-b972-a7f966712ae2" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336087 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1089 "q-349765" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1100 "A concert venue is tracking the rate at which guests enter the building. The rate $r(t)$ at which guests arrive is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\sqrt{t+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} guests arrived." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1113 #uuid "a131754b-a4fe-55c0-993a-b216dfd3a19f" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336086 1145 "We're given the **rate of change** in the number of guests, $r(t).$ To find the number of guests that arrived between the times $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\,\\frac{2(t+1)^{3/2}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3+1)^{3/2}-2(0+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, $14$ guests arrived." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336088 13194139535184 true] [17592186336087 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336087 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336087 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336088 13194139535184 true] [17592186336087 1110 #uuid "943d9af1-c345-5fad-9fb1-ae42487951cb" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336088 1034 "14.0" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336088 1111 #uuid "806e8fa2-8a1b-55f9-8e25-eb19f18feea4" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336090 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1089 "q-349771" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1100 "A website is tracking the rate at which new users sign up. The rate $r(t)$ at which users sign up is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\cdot {2}^{t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in users per day, and $t>0$ is the time in days.\n\nBetween $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, to nearest integer,\n{{field-1}} users signed up." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1113 #uuid "0a01a9ef-4113-5c96-9cb0-3af4ac0237c9" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336089 1145 "We are given the signup rate $r(t)$ in users per day. To find the number of users that signed up between the times $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}12\\cdot {2}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={12\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & ={36\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{3}-{2}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{216}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, $312$ users signed up." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336091 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336092 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336092 13194139535184 true] [17592186336090 1110 #uuid "72b67fe2-2cef-5392-b8f4-229634d35295" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336091 1034 "216" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336091 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336091 1111 #uuid "15210147-c88f-56d1-a200-c01adc83666e" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336092 1034 "312" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336092 1111 #uuid "b43db25e-5a81-5937-89e3-1810b7e9a2c1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336094 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1089 "q-349982" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1100 "Engineers are testing a water pump filling a reservoir.\nThey observe that water enters the reservoir at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\sqrt{t+4},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in gallons per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pump was turned on.\nAt the same time, water leaks from the reservoir at a constant rate of $6$ gallons per minute.\n\nIf the reservoir was initially empty, find the expression that gives $V(t),$ the volume of water in the reservoir $t$ minutes after the pump was turned on.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6t-64$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1113 #uuid "013c80a2-1889-52a0-8325-437967b02894" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336093 1145 "Water is entering the reservoir at a rate of $r(t)$ gallons per minute and is leaking at a rate of $6$ gallons per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the volume of water is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{t+4}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}(12\\sqrt{x+4}-6)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. Also, since the reservoir is initially empty, we set $V(0)=0.$\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}12\\sqrt{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={12\\cdot \\frac{2(x+4)^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}-6x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-8(4)^{3/2}-6t \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-6t-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336095 13194139535184 true] [17592186336094 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336094 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336094 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336095 13194139535184 true] [17592186336094 1110 #uuid "be2e369f-7e27-552c-b6a7-966e138c296c" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336095 1034 "8(t+4)^{3/2}" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336095 1111 #uuid "3683d4f0-3594-5b70-b1f4-51c97bc23549" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336097 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1089 "q-349983" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1100 "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1113 #uuid "8b26926d-a4cd-5562-8160-9ecf1e52b47d" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336096 1145 "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336097 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336098 13194139535184 true] [17592186336097 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336097 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336097 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336098 13194139535184 true] [17592186336097 1110 #uuid "2f4af640-6fc4-561c-9b7f-ce62899e2c1b" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336098 1034 "260-3t" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336098 1111 #uuid "cb0759ef-2195-5981-bac6-3f816302add9" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336100 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336103 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1089 "q-349996" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1100 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1113 #uuid "176edced-7cbc-51f3-b6f0-1dfe994048e9" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336099 1145 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336101 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336102 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336102 13194139535184 true] [17592186336100 1110 #uuid "6abb0c3f-7763-55a7-8885-b51a45a0ee80" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336101 1034 "128" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336101 1111 #uuid "ba55c7e1-1193-5d21-bdbb-9efa3dcd4c2a" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336102 1034 "188" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336102 1111 #uuid "6d5919cb-6213-59cd-ac32-000a5f4b200d" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336104 13194139535184 true] [17592186336103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336103 1041 "field-2" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336104 13194139535184 true] [17592186336103 1110 #uuid "4db061f9-7eb7-5fdd-b245-e885ae3fc59a" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336104 1034 "4" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336104 1111 #uuid "1c58c9e5-6567-5e52-9805-a72c25e59b59" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336106 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336109 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1089 "q-349998" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1100 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1113 #uuid "04d94105-9d71-5769-92eb-b32c2d959987" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336105 1145 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(6-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n5(6-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=3,$ we figure out $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =50+5{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+5{[6x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =50+5[(6\\cdot 3-{3}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =50+5\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $50<95$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $95$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336107 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336108 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1041 "field-1" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336108 13194139535184 true] [17592186336106 1110 #uuid "c6828622-ccf7-5950-8053-3c586f9d3dba" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336107 1034 "50" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336107 1111 #uuid "77316c20-7e57-5ba1-8460-4790f097909a" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336108 1034 "95" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336108 1111 #uuid "476d9191-f297-515c-91f8-2d46c90d9f09" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336110 13194139535184 true] [17592186336109 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535184 true] [17592186336109 1041 "field-2" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336109 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336110 13194139535184 true] [17592186336109 1110 #uuid "5a2004e8-77c6-580f-97d5-1bffa6e85a9d" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336110 1034 "3" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336110 1111 #uuid "f55c4838-3d0f-5365-9107-79b9132bea42" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336112 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1089 "q-62803" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1100 "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1113 #uuid "6d5889d4-4ad2-5352-a1b9-05ab5c8271c8" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336111 1145 "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336113 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336114 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336115 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336116 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336117 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336116 13194139535184 true] [17592186336112 1110 #uuid "46101e9a-4ad7-572e-b200-2c703b884679" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336113 1034 "$$402\\,000$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336113 1111 #uuid "f2a1c8b7-76c4-5c9d-99cb-a2ea596f9766" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336114 1034 "$$176\\,000$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336114 1111 #uuid "d3a87bff-3280-5f01-a29e-80b75cff73ae" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336115 1034 "$$185\\,000$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336115 1111 #uuid "42557b93-ee36-5620-a129-49c0669d5e75" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336116 1034 "$$181\\,000$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336116 1111 #uuid "199f4fdd-1033-5100-ac09-fdfa74ed8a40" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336117 1034 "$$291\\,000$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336117 1111 #uuid "0179ded7-7fbb-57c8-88ff-0a09a373a653" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336119 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1089 "q-62808" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1100 "Air is being pumped into a tire. The rate $r(t)$ at which air enters the tire is given by a function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{500}{1+2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},$ and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. Initially, there was $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of air in the tire.\n\nWhich integral gives the volume of air in the tire $t$ minutes since the pumping started?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1113 #uuid "e5e01b2b-59f7-5c9b-a441-1c8b40304b9a" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336118 1145 "We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$ To find the **total volume** after $t$ minutes (call it $V(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336120 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336121 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336122 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336123 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336124 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336123 13194139535184 true] [17592186336119 1110 #uuid "f4618cc4-ad1a-58f8-883f-8095cb379739" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336120 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{50}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336120 1111 #uuid "5abaf428-9128-5508-9dd9-aa296fc9bc1f" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336121 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336121 1111 #uuid "a82a9ab8-f33c-5a5a-9c74-3e91f5887df5" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336122 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{t}(50+\\frac{500}{1+2x})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336122 1111 #uuid "8ab46693-d9e9-5bbc-ab14-8c8ccf17fcfa" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336123 1034 "50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336123 1111 #uuid "d723c0b9-b110-5cdb-b094-294e63aef077" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336124 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{50}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535184 true] [17592186336124 1111 #uuid "f204ac6e-eeec-5e73-8706-12bcea70a8ee" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336126 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1089 "q-62825" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1100 "A tank has an inlet and an outlet. When the inlet is turned on, the rate at which water flows into the tank governed by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=9{t}^{2}+18,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nWhen the outlet is switched on, water leaves the tank through it at a rate of $14$ liters per minute.\n\nThe inlet is switched on, and the water level increases to $120$ liters, at which point the outlet is also switched on. How much water is in the tank $10$ minutes after switching on the outlet, given that the outlet is switched on at $t=0?$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1113 #uuid "d586afc6-dd55-5a3f-a72a-36aae91b1948" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336125 1145 "Water enters the tank at a rate of $9{t}^{2}+18$ liters per minute and leaves at a rate of $14$ liters per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change of volume** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+18-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total volume** of water in the tank after $t$ minutes (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the volume of water after $10$ minutes, we substitute $t=10$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(10) & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{10}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{[3{x}^{3}+4x]|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =120+[(3(10)^{3}+4(10))-(0)] \\\\  & =120+3040 \\\\  & =3\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336127 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336128 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336129 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336130 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336131 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336131 13194139535184 true] [17592186336126 1110 #uuid "da9ee08b-d711-541b-924d-6a8909b280fa" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336127 1034 "$3\\,020$ liters" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336127 1111 #uuid "f43cd29c-fa96-5b19-9eab-68c6b962799c" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336128 1034 "$3\\,140$ liters" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336128 1111 #uuid "c9353486-e1ee-598a-bb98-f05c8cad5b32" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336129 1034 "$350$ liters" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336129 1111 #uuid "d59deb8e-814c-501a-a210-68f42f7751ea" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336130 1034 "$250$ liters" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336130 1111 #uuid "fcd9947c-2fac-56af-a9a4-4bc16fab8efd" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336131 1034 "$3\\,160$ liters" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336131 1111 #uuid "fa29857f-bd00-5600-80b8-f5b60ceaeee8" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336133 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1089 "q-90694" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1100 "Since $2005$, the population of a certain town grows at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n \n\n$$\nr(t)=200{e}^{0.2t}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years since $2005.$\n\nTo the nearest integer, by how many people did the population of the town increase between the years $2010$ and $2015?$" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1113 #uuid "a4a96f45-52c2-52f7-a934-05bbe7001e15" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336132 1145 "We're given the **rate of change** in population $r(t).$ To find the change in  population between  $2010$ and $2015$, we integrate $r(t)$ between the$t=5$ and $t=10.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{10}200{e}^{0.2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =200\\cdot {{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,{{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & ≈4\\,671\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.  \n\nTherefore, between  $2010$ and $2015$ the population of the town increased by $4\\,671$ people." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336134 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336135 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336136 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336137 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336138 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336138 13194139535184 true] [17592186336133 1110 #uuid "08d1c4ae-a1b0-52f6-82a6-021f81d038d7" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336134 1034 "$6\\,471$ people" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336134 1111 #uuid "414b0cc8-fd63-5f5f-b31f-a1de4efa6174" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336135 1034 "$1\\,467$ people" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336135 1111 #uuid "2b6e9c1f-3046-5b81-a670-b6b672045f9c" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336136 1034 "$1\\,764$ people" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336136 1111 #uuid "400205ac-0e74-5985-bb4a-66b94fb18dd5" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336137 1034 "$7\\,461$ people" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336137 1111 #uuid "7d795093-08f1-5af2-977f-da15b141598d" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336138 1034 "$4\\,671$ people" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336138 1111 #uuid "84975ce5-cf1a-5175-8980-b8d402dd8c78" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336140 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1089 "q-90756" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1100 "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1113 #uuid "3e88d227-68c0-5aae-9197-414a3be89369" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336139 1145 "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336141 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336142 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336143 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336144 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336145 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336144 13194139535184 true] [17592186336140 1110 #uuid "041d2d0e-c5b1-5127-beca-eddf31b90a74" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336141 1034 "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336141 1111 #uuid "033ff1f2-bc5c-526d-bf0c-e98c809d74d2" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336142 1034 "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336142 1111 #uuid "d298d34e-e56c-5372-8be8-374d47c321bf" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336143 1034 "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336143 1111 #uuid "d11448c1-7a0b-5332-8af2-5f845bd25322" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336144 1034 "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336144 1111 #uuid "7e8957a5-f12f-5e4f-bdd3-4b6816480cf5" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336145 1034 "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336145 1111 #uuid "08b6b69c-17a2-55fb-9c59-8d94ac8cd910" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336147 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1089 "q-90758" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1100 "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1113 #uuid "c2c8878a-6a18-5df8-8fae-915f579f14bc" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336146 1145 "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336148 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336149 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336150 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336151 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336152 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1041 "selection" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336151 13194139535184 true] [17592186336147 1110 #uuid "6af206e8-9d4e-5b40-a179-d79100d3eb32" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336148 1034 "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336148 1111 #uuid "76313753-5b0b-5afa-9883-ea2939d5b8af" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336149 1034 "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535184 true] [17592186336149 1111 #uuid "a684a165-a5c0-57ef-b646-8a277848e61b" 13194139535184 true] [17592186336